// A state which reduces on a token it does not accept hides the tokens it does accept, unless the parser checks the
// lookahead before it reduces. The state after the name "b" shifts "(" for a call and reduces the name to an expression
// on ")", because the same state is reached inside an argument list, where ")" does follow an expression. The default
// reduction extends that to every other token as well.
//
// Reducing on the ")" would reduce the expression and the assignment and report the error in a state which expects only
// the end of input, so the "(" which would have continued the input would be missing from the message. Lookahead
// correction tries the reductions on the side first. They end in an error, so the error is reported right after the name
// was shifted, with the name still on the stack, and the message lists both "(" and the end of input. A backend which
// shows a REDUCE line before the ERROR does not check the lookahead before reducing.
//
// The scanner is not what this case is about, so its rules are single characters and runs of letters.

@scanner {
    WHITESPACE: /[ \t\n]+/ @skip;

    NAME:   /[a-z]+/;
    ASSIGN: "=";
    LPAREN: "(";
    RPAREN: ")";
    COMMA:  ",";
}

@parser {
    assignment
        : NAME "=" expression
        ;

    expression
        : NAME
        | NAME "(" arguments ")"
        ;

    arguments
        : expression
        | arguments "," expression
        ;
}
